# Every branch edits TODO.md, and every branch edits it in the same two places:
# a line appended at the head of Now when the work starts, and the same line
# moved to the head of Done when the pull request merges. A three-way merge
# calls two branches inserting at one anchor a conflict, which is why nearly
# every pull request had to resolve this file by hand.
#
# The union driver keeps both sides' lines instead of asking. The price is that
# it is silent: move a line to Done while another branch appends beside it and
# union can leave the Now copy in place, so the item sits in both sections at
# once with no marker to show for it. src/lib/todo-list.test.ts fails the build
# on an item whose words appear twice — the only thing standing between this
# line and a list that quietly lies.
#
# It also reaches git and nothing else. GitHub does not apply merge drivers from
# this file, so its mergeability check re-derives the conflict the union driver
# just resolved, and a pull request whose merge is clean still shows "This
# branch has conflicts that must be resolved" the moment another branch touches
# TODO.md. There is nothing to resolve: merge main locally, where the driver
# applies, hand-correct Now if todo-list.test.ts fires, and push. The banner
# clears. To see the difference for yourself:
#
#   git merge-tree --write-tree --messages HEAD origin/main
#   git -c attr.tree=<a commit before this rule> merge-tree --write-tree \
#       --messages HEAD origin/main
#
# The first auto-merges. The second is what GitHub sees.
TODO.md merge=union
